Third-party data
----------------

- "ER - Motion Values and Attack Data (App Ver. 1.17)", a community spreadsheet
  of Elden Ring motion values and attack data. The Vanilla snapshot's skill
  attack, route, stamina and poise tables are derived from it, and a copy is
  kept in data/phase1/ as an extraction input. Credit belongs to its author;
  the workbook's Info sheet links to its source and archived versions.
- tclark.io, the Elden Ring weapon calculator by T. Clark (ThomasJClark)
  (https://eldenring.tclark.io, source at
  https://github.com/ThomasJClark/elden-ring-weapon-calculator). The Convergence
  availability reference in data/reference/convergence-3.0.0.1-weapons.json is
  derived from its regulation-convergence-v3.0.0.1.js data, and the validation
  tools compare results against a pinned version of its source.
- Paramdex parameter definitions and names (distributed with WitchyBND) are used
  during extraction; neither is redistributed here.

Rights in these sources remain with their authors.

Third-party software
--------------------

The desktop executable statically links Rust crates and bundles npm packages
under their own licenses. The full lists, with versions, are in
apps/desktop/src-tauri/Cargo.lock and apps/desktop/package-lock.json; each
package's license text is in its published source.

- Rust crates are mostly MIT OR Apache-2.0. Others include Unicode-3.0 (the ICU4X
  crates), BSD-3-Clause (brotli, alloc-no-stdlib, alloc-stdlib), Zlib (foldhash)
  and MPL-2.0. The MPL-2.0 crates are cssparser, cssparser-macros, selectors,
  dtoa-short and option-ext; they are used unmodified, and their source is
  available from https://crates.io.
- Bundled npm packages: react, react-dom, scheduler and zustand (MIT),
  lucide-react (ISC) and @tauri-apps/api (Apache-2.0 OR MIT).
